   <pb xml:id="leaf001r" facs="loc_nk.00530_large.jpg" type="recto"/>
   
   <note hand="#h02" type="authorial" place="top">169</note>
   
   <note hand="#h02" type="authorial" place="top">Elias Hicks</note>
   
   <p>
    <del rend="overstrike">To conclude</del>
    &#182;I don't know in what book I
    once read, (<subst hand="#h02"><del rend="overstrike" seq="1">probably</del><add place="above" seq="2">possibly</add></subst> the remark has
    been <add place="below"><metamark target="#a001" function="add">&#8248;</metamark></add><del rend="overstrike"><add place="above" xml:id="a001"><unclear reason="illegible" resp="#ja" cert="high">controversially</unclear></add></del> made in books all ages,) that no
    life ever lived, even the most uneventful,
    but probed to its centre would be
    found <add place="below"><metamark target="#a002" function="add">&#8248;</metamark></add><add place="above" xml:id="a002">in itself</add> a subtle<subst><del rend="overstrike" seq="1">r</del><add place="above" seq="2">r</add></subst> drama <del rend="overstrike" seq="1">in itself</del><del rend="overstrike" seq="3"><add place="above" seq="2">far</add></del><add place="above" seq="4">as <gap reason="illegible" extent="one word" resp="#ja"/></add> tha<subst><del rend="overwrite" seq="1">n</del><add place="over" rend="overwrite" seq="2" hand="#h03">t</add></subst>
    poets <del rend="overstrike" hand="#h02">yet</del> have <add place="above" hand="#h02">ever</add> sung or playwrights fabled.
    Often, too, in <del rend="overstrike">real</del> size and weight. <subst><del rend="overstrike" seq="1">It</del><add place="above" seq="2">For</add></subst>
    isn't only the <subst><del rend="overstrike" seq="1">visible</del> <add place="below" seq="2"><metamark target="#a003" function="add">&#8248;</metamark></add><add place="above" seq="3" xml:id="a003"><del rend="overstrike" seq="4">celebrated</del><add place="above" seq="5">palpable</add></add></subst> <del rend="overstrike" seq="1">orbs</del> <add place="below" seq="2"><metamark target="#a004" function="add">&#8248;</metamark></add><add place="above" seq="3" xml:id="a004">stars:</add> astronomers
    say, there are dark or almost dark <subst><add place="below" seq="2"><metamark target="#a005" function="add" >&#8248;</metamark></add><add place="above" xml:id="a005" seq="3">unnoticed orbs</add> <del rend="overstrike" seq="1">stars</del></subst>
    and suns, (like the dusky companion
    of Sirius, seven times as large as our
    own sun) rolling through space, <add place="below" seq="2"><metamark target="#a006" function="add">&#8248;</metamark></add><add place="above" xml:id="a006" seq="3"></add> <del rend="overstrike" seq="1">as</del> real
    and potent. Only none <unclear reason="illegible" resp="#ja" cert="low">reeks</unclear> of them. In the
    bright <gap extent="one word" resp="#ja" reason="illegible"/> we gave the sky, they have
    not even names. Amid the sophistications
    of <unclear reason="illegible" resp="#ja" cert="high">life</unclear> the soul seems to <gap reason="illegible" resp="#ja" extent="one word"/> yearningly around
    for such contrasts such cool stable <unclear reason="illegible" resp="#ja" cert="low">offices</unclear>. And
    now for one <gap reason="illegible" resp="#ja" extent="one word"/>.
   </p>
   
